  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "ISCII and Unicode"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "ASCII cannot represent Indian scripts. ISCII (Indian Script Code for Information Interchange) is an 8-bit code with 256 codes; it keeps the 128 ASCII characters and uses the remaining codes for Indian scripts such as Devanagari, Kannada and Tamil. Unicode is a universal standard that gives a unique code point to every character of every major language. Its encodings include UTF-8 (1 to 4 bytes per character), UTF-16 and UTF-32 (4 bytes per character). The first 128 Unicode characters are the same as ASCII."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "ISCII: 8 bits → 256 codes, Indian scripts"
    },
    {
     "en": "Unicode: one standard for all languages"
    },
    {
     "en": "UTF-8 uses 1–4 bytes; UTF-32 uses 4 bytes"
    },
    {
     "en": "ASCII is a subset of Unicode"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "ASCII cannot represent Indian scripts. ISCII (Indian Script Code for Information Interchange) is an 8-bit code with 256 codes; it keeps the 128 ASCII characters and uses the remaining codes for Indian scripts such as Devanagari, Kannada and Tamil. Unicode is a universal standard that gives a unique code point to every character of every major language. Its encodings include UTF-8 (1 to 4 bytes per character), UTF-16 and UTF-32 (4 bytes per character). The first 128 Unicode characters are the same as ASCII.",
    "kn": "ASCII ಭಾರತೀಯ ಲಿಪಿಗಳನ್ನು ಪ್ರತಿನಿಧಿಸಲಾರದು. ISCII 8-ಬಿಟ್ ಸಂಕೇತ; 256 ಸಂಕೇತಗಳಿವೆ, ಭಾರತೀಯ ಲಿಪಿಗಳಿಗೆ ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಯೂನಿಕೋಡ್ ಜಗತ್ತಿನ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಭಾಷೆಗಳ ಪ್ರತಿ ಅಕ್ಷರಕ್ಕೂ ವಿಶಿಷ್ಟ ಸಂಕೇತ ನೀಡುವ ಸಾರ್ವತ್ರಿಕ ಮಾನದಂಡ. UTF-8, UTF-16, UTF-32 ಅದರ ರೂಪಗಳು."
   }
  },
